/* libjbn profile: x86-64 with BMI2 and ADX (Intel Broadwell and later, AMD
   Zen and later; not under Rosetta). Required once, at the top level of a
   program, before the instances (README, "Profiles and the selection of
   the multiplication").

   JBN_FEATURES:   BMI2 and ADX, so the multiplication rows use
                   MULX/ADCX/ADOX.
   JBN_REG_MAX:    up to this size, the multiplication, squaring and
                   Montgomery reduction (bn_mulsel@n.jinc) are those of
                   bn_mulxr, whose rows keep their accumulator in registers
                   (at most 6: above, its reduction does not fit them).
                   5: on an Intel Core i7-14700K, bn_mulxr is faster than
                   bn_mulx at 4 and 5 limbs (fpc_mul, fpc_sqr: 6% to 12%
                   with independent calls; chained, 2% to 5% at 4 limbs,
                   equal at 5). At 6 limbs it is faster with independent
                   calls only (5% to 8%), and 2% to 4% slower on an
                   exponentiation. A size scope whose field
                   multiplications are mostly independent may shadow it
                   with 6.
   JBN_UNROLL_MAX: the multiplication, squaring and Montgomery reduction of
                   a size scope (bn_mulsel@n.jinc) are unrolled when
                   nlimbs <= JBN_UNROLL_MAX, and loop over their rows above.
                   16: on an Intel Core i7-14700K the unrolled bn_mulx is 8%
                   to 11% faster than the looped bn_mulx_loop on an
                   exponentiation up to 16 limbs, and 59% slower at 32 limbs,
                   where its code no longer fits the 32 KB instruction cache.
                   Sizes between 16 and 32 limbs were not timed; the
                   crossover is expected near 24 limbs on that CPU, and
                   depends on the size of the instruction cache.
   JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX: the squaring of a size scope is the unrolled one up
                   to this size, also where the multiplication and the
                   reduction loop (above JBN_UNROLL_MAX): the looped module
                   squares by multiplying.
                   40: on an Intel Core i7-14700K, with the looped
                   multiplication and reduction, the unrolled squaring
                   makes fpc_sqr 19% to 21% faster and an exponentiation
                   14% to 17% faster at 24, 32 and 40 limbs, and both 4%
                   slower at 48 limbs, where it has 42 KB (29 KB at 40) and
                   no longer fits the 32 KB instruction cache.
                   Above it, a field bound with fp_mul_ksqr@fp.jinc takes
                   the squaring over the half size (bn_ksqr@2n.jinc), in a
                   size scope that has a half scope: on the same CPU it
                   makes fpc_sqr 20% to 26% faster and an exponentiation
                   14% to 22% faster from 48 to 128 limbs, and it is slower
                   than the unrolled squaring up to 40 limbs (by 2% at 40).
   JBN_EXP_WINDOW: the constant-time exponentiation of a field (_expm_ of
                   bn_exp@exp.jinc): 0 for the Montgomery ladder, which
                   needs no table; otherwise the fixed window of 4 bits,
                   with a table of 16*nlimbs words on the stack.
                   4: on an Intel Core i7-14700K the window takes 0.58 to
                   0.78 of the time of the ladder, for every module and
                   size from 4 to 64 limbs.
   A size scope may shadow JBN_REG_MAX, JBN_UNROLL_MAX, JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX
   and JBN_EXP_WINDOW (`param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 0;` and
   `param int JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX = 0;` inside it, for instance, for the
   smallest code at that size; `param int JBN_EXP_WINDOW = 0;` for the
   smallest stack).
*/

from Libjbn require "jbn_features.jinc"

param int JBN_FEATURES = JBN_FEATURES_MULX;
param int JBN_REG_MAX = 5;
param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 16;
param int JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX = 40;
param int JBN_EXP_WINDOW = 4;
